weyl-curvature-hypothesis-initial-state-.conceptual_definition:
The Weyl Curvature Hypothesis posits that in the very early universe, close to the Big Bang singularity (
$t \to 0$
), the Weyl tensor, a component of the Riemann curvature tensor that describes tidal forces and gravitational waves, tends to zero. This implies that the universe was initially highly symmetric and free from gravitational perturbations, essentially being a 'smooth' spacetime. As the universe evolves and time progresses (
$t > 0$
), the Weyl tensor can grow, leading to the complex structures and gravitational phenomena we observe today. This hypothesis is a key concept in understanding the initial conditions of the universe and the origin of structure formation.
